\begin_layout Plain Layout

\series bold
Euler Equation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\noindent
\align left
\begin_inset Formula $u^{\prime}[c_{t}]=\beta u^{\prime}[c_{t+1}]f^{\prime}(k_{t+1})$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0,\ldots,T-1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula $k_{T+1}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*

\bar under
Problem 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider an economy with two price-taking firms.
 Firm 1 has a production technology 
\begin_inset Formula $f(k_{1},n_{1})=k_{1}^{\frac{1}{3}}n_{1}^{\frac{2}{3}}+k_{1}$
\end_inset

, while firm 2 has 
\begin_inset Formula $g(k_{2},n_{2})=k_{2}^{\frac{2}{3}}n_{2}^{\frac{1}{3}}+n_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose the total supply of capital is 
\begin_inset Formula $(k_{1}+k_{2}=5)$
\end_inset

, and the total supply of labor is 
\begin_inset Formula $(n_{1}+n_{2}=3)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
(a) Formulate the equilibrium condition as a system of non-linear equations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Profit function for Firm 1:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{1}=k_{1}^{\frac{1}{3}}n_{1}^{\frac{2}{3}}+k_{1}-R\cdot k_{1}-W\cdot n_{1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
First order conditions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial\Pi_{1}}{\partial n_{1}}=\frac{2}{3}k_{1}^{\frac{1}{3}}n_{1}^{-\frac{1}{3}}-W=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longleftrightarrow\frac{2}{3}k_{1}^{\frac{1}{3}}n_{1}^{-\frac{1}{3}}=W$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longleftrightarrow\frac{k_{1}}{n_{1}}=\frac{27W^{3}}{8}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longleftrightarrow\frac{n_{1}}{k_{1}}=\frac{8}{27W^{3}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial\Pi_{1}}{\partial k_{1}}=\frac{1}{3}k_{1}^{-\frac{2}{3}}n_{1}^{\frac{2}{3}}+1-R=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longleftrightarrow\frac{1}{3}k_{1}^{-\frac{2}{3}}n_{1}^{\frac{2}{3}}+1=R$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longleftrightarrow k_{1}^{-\frac{2}{3}}n_{1}^{\frac{2}{3}}=3(R-1)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longleftrightarrow\frac{n_{1}}{k_{1}}=[3(R-1)]^{\frac{3}{2}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Combine the two equations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{8}{27W^{3}}=[3(R-1)]^{\frac{3}{2}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longleftrightarrow\frac{4}{9W^{2}}=R^{*}-1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Profit function for Firm 2:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{2}=k_{2}^{\frac{2}{3}}n_{2}^{\frac{1}{3}}+n_{2}-R\cdot k_{2}-W\cdot n_{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
First order conditions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial\Pi_{1}}{\partial k_{2}}=\frac{2}{3}k_{2}^{-\frac{1}{3}}n_{2}^{\frac{1}{3}}-R=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longleftrightarrow\frac{2}{3}k_{2}^{-\frac{1}{3}}n_{2}^{\frac{1}{3}}=R$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longleftrightarrow\frac{n_{2}}{k_{2}}=\frac{27R^{3}}{8}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longleftrightarrow\frac{k_{2}}{n_{2}}=\frac{8}{27R^{3}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial\Pi_{1}}{\partial n_{2}}=\frac{1}{3}k_{2}^{\frac{2}{3}}n_{1}^{-\frac{2}{3}}+1-W=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longleftrightarrow\frac{1}{3}k_{1}^{\frac{2}{3}}n_{1}^{-\frac{2}{3}}+1=W$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longleftrightarrow k_{2}^{\frac{2}{3}}n_{2}^{-\frac{2}{3}}=3(W-1)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longleftrightarrow\frac{k_{2}}{n_{2}}=[3(W-1)]^{\frac{3}{2}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Combine the two equations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{8}{27R^{3}}=[3(W-1)]^{\frac{3}{2}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longleftrightarrow\frac{4}{9R^{2}}=W^{*}-1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
System of non-linear equations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none
\begin_inset Formula $R^{*}=\frac{4}{9W}+1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $W^{*}=\frac{4}{9R^{2}}+1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
(b) Use Newton's Method to find the equilibrium allocations and price levels.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, we can substitute the equation for 
\begin_inset Formula $W^{*}$
\end_inset

 into the equation for 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{*}$
\end_inset

 to obtain the following function:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $f(R^{*})=\frac{4}{27\left(\frac{4}{27R^{2}}+1\right)^{2}}+1-R^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The derivate of this function with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{*}$
\end_inset

 is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $f^{\prime}(R^{*})=-\frac{8}{27}\left(\frac{4}{27}R^{*^{2}}+1\right)^{-3}\left(-\frac{8}{27}R^{*^{-3}}\right)-1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, to find a solution to 
\begin_inset Formula $f(R^{*})=0$
\end_inset

, we use Newton's method.
 We use the following MATLAB code, with 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{initial}^{*}=1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $n=50$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $eps=0.0000001$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Box Frameless
position "t"
hor_pos "c"
has_inner_box 1
inner_pos "t"
use_parbox 0
use_makebox 0
width "100col%"
special "none"
height "1in"
height_special "totalheight"
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
function f=Newton(R,n,eps) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

for i=1:n 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

f = -R+1+(4/(27*(1+(4/(27*R^2)))^2)); 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

derf = -2*(4/27)*(((4/27)*(R^-2)+1)^-3)*(-2*(4/27)*R^(-3))-1; 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Rnew = R-(f/derf); 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

if abs(Rnew - R) < eps 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

break; 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

else 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

R = Rnew; 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

end 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

end 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Rnew 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
end
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After the program runs, the equilibrium price of capital is: 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{*}=1.1184$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can take this value, and plug it into the equation for 
\begin_inset Formula $W^{*}$
\end_inset

 to obtain the equilibrium price of labor: 
\begin_inset Formula $W^{*}=1.1184$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Next, we obtain the equilibrium allocations for capital using the following
 equations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{n_{1}}{k_{1}}=\frac{8}{27W^{3}}\Longrightarrow n_{1}=k_{1}\cdot\frac{8}{27W^{*^{3}}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{n_{2}}{k_{2}}=\frac{27R^{3}}{8}\Longrightarrow n_{2}=k_{2}\cdot\frac{27R^{*^{3}}}{8}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}+k_{2}=5\Longrightarrow k_{1}=5-k_{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, substitute this information into the total supply of labor:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $n_{1}+n_{2}=3$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longrightarrow\left[(5-k_{2})\cdot\frac{8}{27W^{*^{3}}}\right]+\left[k_{2}\cdot\frac{27R^{*^{3}}}{8}\right]=3$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longrightarrow\frac{40}{27(1.1184)^{3}}-\frac{8}{27(1.1184)^{3}}k_{2}+\frac{27(1.1184)^{3}}{8}k_{2}=3$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longrightarrow\frac{729(1.1184)^{6}-64}{216(1.1184)^{3}}k_{2}=3-\frac{40}{27(1.1184)^{3}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longrightarrow k_{2}^{*}\approx0.43042$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally, we obtain the equilibrium allocations for capital using the following
 equations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{k_{1}}{n_{1}}=\frac{27W^{*^{3}}}{8}\Longrightarrow k_{1}=n_{1}\cdot\frac{27W^{*^{3}}}{8}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{k_{2}}{n_{2}}=\frac{8}{27R^{*^{3}}}\Longrightarrow k_{2}=n_{2}\cdot\frac{8}{27R^{*^{3}}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $n_{1}+n_{2}=3\Longrightarrow n_{1}=3-n_{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, substitute this information into the total supply of capital:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}+k_{2}=5$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longrightarrow\left[\left(3-n_{2}\right)\cdot\frac{27W^{*^{3}}}{8}\right]+\left[n_{2}\cdot\frac{8}{27R^{*^{3}}}\right]=5$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longrightarrow\frac{81(1.1184)^{3}}{8}-\frac{27(1.1184)^{3}}{8}n_{2}+\frac{8}{27(1.1184)^{3}}n_{2}=5$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longrightarrow\frac{64-729(1.1184)^{6}}{216(1.1184)^{3}}n_{2}=5-\frac{81(1.1184)^{3}}{8}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Longrightarrow n_{2}^{*}\approx2.0321$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, the equilibrium allocations and price levels are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}^{*}\approx4.56958$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $k_{2}^{*}\approx0.43042$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $n_{1}^{*}\approx0.9679$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $n_{2}^{*}\approx2.0321$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $R^{*}=W^{*}=1.1184$
\end_inset


\end_layout

